Package: gettoken-contract-defs
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: token broker for AI agents - the shapes every other contract is built from
 An agent name, an activity, a capability, a signature, a key, a secret, a
 version, a lifetime. Nothing crosses a boundary except as one of these, and a
 contract that admits a field says which of them it is.

Package: gettoken-contract-agent-capability-request
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - what an agent says when it asks for a capability
 Carries the capability wanted and nothing else. An agent cannot say who it
 is or what it is doing; the privileged half decides that.

Package: gettoken-contract-agent-list-request
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: token broker for AI agents - what an agent says when it asks what it may equip
 Carries the word list and nothing else, so asking what is available is not a
 way to ask for anything.

Package: gettoken-contract-entitlements-request
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - what the privileged half asks entitlements
 Carries who is asking and what they are doing, signed by the privileged
 half. It names no capability, because none is being asked for.

Package: gettoken-contract-entitlements-response
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - what entitlements answers
 Every capability the agent may equip, each saying what it is for and what
 has to be filled in before it can be asked for.

Package: gettoken-contract-exchange-request
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - what token-service hands an exchanger
 Carries who is asking and what for. It does not carry the signature the
 request was signed with, and it does not carry what the agent was doing: an
 exchanger is told the least that lets it trade.

Package: gettoken-contract-secret-get-request
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - asking the store for the newest secret under a key
 Carries the key alone. The store answers with the version it holds, so no
 caller has to know which one that is.

Package: gettoken-contract-secret-get-request-version
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - asking the store for one version under a key
 Carries the key and the version wanted, for a caller that needs the one it
 read before rather than the newest.

Package: gettoken-contract-secret-get-response
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - what the store answers with a secret
 Carries the secret and the version it is. Nothing says whether anything was
 found, because the exit status says that.

Package: gettoken-contract-secret-put-request
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - putting a secret in the store
 Carries the key and the secret. It carries no version: the store assigns
 that, so no caller can overwrite what is already held.

Package: gettoken-contract-secret-put-response
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - what the store answers when it stored a secret
 Carries the secret and the version the store gave it. A caller that put a
 secret in gets back the version it was assigned, so it can ask for that one
 again later rather than for whatever is newest by then.

Package: gettoken-contract-token-request
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - what the privileged half asks token-service
 Carries who is asking, what they are doing, what they want, and the
 signature the privileged half vouches with.

Package: gettoken-contract-token-response
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract-defs
Description: token broker for AI agents - what token-service answers with a token
 Carries the token and how long it lasts. A lifetime is bounded at both ends,
 so neither a token that expires before it is used nor one that outlives the
 job is admitted.

Package: gettoken-parse
Architecture: any
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: token broker for AI agents - reads a document through its contract
 parse reads one document on standard input, checks it against the contract
 named on its command line, and hands back the fields asked for as assignments
 a shell can evaluate. A document that does not satisfy its contract yields
 nothing to evaluate.
 .
 It carries no contract of its own. What may cross a boundary is stated by the
 contract package the component reading that boundary depends on.

Package: gettoken-format
Architecture: any
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: token broker for AI agents - writes a document through its contract
 format takes the fields named on its command line from the environment, writes
 the document they make, and checks that document against the contract before
 handing it over. A component cannot emit something its contract does not admit.
 .
 It carries no contract of its own, for the same reason parse does not.

Package: gettoken
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-parse,
         gettoken-format,
         gettoken-entitlements,
         gettoken-token-service,
         gettoken-contract-agent-capability-request,
         gettoken-contract-agent-list-request,
         gettoken-contract-entitlements-request,
         gettoken-contract-entitlements-response,
         gettoken-contract-token-request,
         gettoken-contract-token-response
Description: token broker for AI agents
 An agent that holds a long-lived credential holds whatever authority that
 credential carries, and an agent can be talked into things by whatever it
 reads. gettoken hands an agent a token narrow enough for the job in front of
 it and nothing more, drawn from a super-token a human already holds.
 .
 This package contains the entry point the agent invokes and the privileged
 half that builds the request on its behalf. The agent never holds the
 super-token: it says what it wants, and gets back a token for that job.

Package: gettoken-token-service
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-parse,
         gettoken-format,
         gettoken-secret-manager,
         gettoken-contract-token-request,
         gettoken-contract-token-response,
         gettoken-contract-exchange-request
Description: token broker for AI agents - exchange dispatcher
 token-service authenticates a request, resolves what it asks for, and hands
 the capability to the exchanger registered for the service named by the
 capability's first segment.
 .
 It ships no exchanger. Each integrated tool installs its own into
 /usr/lib/gettoken/exchangers, so adding the hundredth tool costs what the
 first one did.

Package: gettoken-entitlements
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-parse,
         gettoken-format,
         gettoken-contract-entitlements-request,
         gettoken-contract-entitlements-response
Description: token broker for AI agents - capability inventory
 entitlements answers what an agent has to work with. It is what
 "gettoken --list" shows, and it is also the control: a request for a
 capability it does not list is refused.

Package: gettoken-secret-manager
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-parse,
         gettoken-format,
         gettoken-contract-secret-get-request,
         gettoken-contract-secret-get-request-version,
         gettoken-contract-secret-get-response,
         gettoken-contract-secret-put-request,
         gettoken-contract-secret-put-response
Description: token broker for AI agents - super-token store
 The store holds the super-tokens a human placed on the privileged side,
 keyed by who holds them and which service they are for, and keeps every
 version that was put in.
 .
 The store is /var/lib/gettoken/secrets, and no account but the privileged one
 can read it. Nothing an agent runs reaches it; only an exchanger does, and
 only for as long as it takes to trade a super-token for a narrow one. Purging
 this package takes the store with it.

Package: integration-test-tool
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken,
         gettoken-parse,
         gettoken-format,
         gettoken-contract-exchange-request,
         gettoken-contract-secret-get-request,
         gettoken-contract-secret-get-response,
         gettoken-contract-token-response
Description: token broker for AI agents - tool that verifies the chain
 The tool the verification suite integrates, standing in for a real one so the
 chain can be exercised without reaching a service. It runs on the token that
 integrationtest/ci/run downgrades to and refuses the super-token that token
 was traded for, so a run that succeeds is proof that a downgrade happened.
 .
 It is also the worked example of an integration: it depends on gettoken and
 carries one exchanger, which knows one service and nothing about any other.
 What that exchanger may say and be told is the contracts it depends on, and
 nothing else. Installing it is what brings the chain onto a machine.
